ion":"NONE","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Tested versions\n\nTested in Godot Mono 4.5.1\n\n### System information\n\nGodot v4.5.1.stable.mono - Windows 11 (build 26200) - Multi-window, 1 monitor - Direct3D 12 (Forward+) - dedicated NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3060 (NVIDIA; 32.0.15.8157) - AMD Ryzen 5 3600 6-Core Processor (12 threads) - 31.92 GiB memory\n\n### Issue description\n\nI had started to migrate my project from GDScript to C# when I encountered some problems with a very simple script\n\n```\nusing Godot;\n\n[GlobalClass]\npublic partial class OneshotEmitter : GpuParticles3D\n{\n\tpublic void OnFinished()\n\t{\n\t\tQueueFree();\n\t}\n}\n```\n\nSimply put, it refuses to register as a global class. No matter what I do, OneshotEmitter will not appear in node selection list. The file is correctly named OneshotEmitter.cs, and other classes register just fine. The only way for this one to appear is if I change the parent class to any other\n\nMoreover, if I create a GPUParticles3D node and add the script to it by hand, when I look at it in the remote tree viewer while the game runs, it has no icon, although otherwise it works\n\nMy gut feeling says the problems arise from the fact that its GPUParticles3D in Godot and GpuParticles3D in C#\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1) Create a class inheriting from GpuParticles3D in Mono project and mark it as global\n2) Attempt to add it as a node\n\n### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)\n\nn/a","closed_by":{"login":"AThousandShips","id":96648715,"node_id":"U_kgDOBcK-Cw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/96648715?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/AThousandShips","html_url":"https://github.com/AThousandShips","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AThousandShips/followers","foll